C o m p i l a t i o n


      Maddie Witte
      USU CEE 6410
      Homework 5
      October 19, 2026
       
      Problem Statement
       
      Two water sources are being considered for a farm: a gravity diversion from reservoir (with 2 possible sizes) or
      a pump from a lower river diversion. Base flow in the river adds 2 acft per day under the reservoir and above the
      pumping site. The high dam has a capacity of 700 acft and the low dam's is 300 acft with costs of $10,000 per
      year for the high dam and $6,000 per year for the low dam. The pump has a capacity of 2.2 acft per day and a yearly
      cost of $8,000 and an operational cost of $20 per acft pumped to the farm.
       
      The return for the farmland is $300 per acre irrigated. The yearly water supply from the river and irrigation
      demand are in the table below; the year is broken up into two 6 mo long seasons.
       
      Seasonal Flow and demand
      season (t)      River Inflow (Q) (acft)      Irrigation Demand (acft/acre)
      1               600                          1.0
      2               200                          3.0
       
      Maximize profits from the system.
       
  26   
  27  Sets
  28      watersource /lowdam, highdam, riverpump/
  29      season /season1, season2/
  30      farmland /area/;
  31   
  32  Parameters
  33      WaterSupply(season)         amount of water supplied to the reservoir via the river (acft)
  34          /season1 600, season2 200/
  35      IrrigationDemand(season)      the amount of water req by the farmland (acft per acre)
  36          /season1 1.0, season2 3.0/
  37      MaxCapacity(watersource)    yearly capactiy of each water source in acft (2.2 acft per day * 365 days per year = 803 acft)
  38          /highdam 700, lowdam 300, riverpump 803/
  39      InitialCost(watersource)    inital cost of each water collection method ($)
  40          /highdam 10000, lowdam 6000, riverpump 8000/
  41      OperatingCost(watersource)  cost to run the dam or pump per acft of water ($)
  42          /highdam 0, lowdam 0, riverpump 20/
  43      Return(farmland)            return per year per acre of farm ($)
  44          /area 300/
  45  ;
  46   
  47  Positive Variables
  48      X(watersource, season)  amount of water pulled from said watersource (ac-ft continuous)
  49      A(farmland)     total area of farmland to be irrigated (ac continous)
  50      S(season)       storage in reservoir between season (acft)
  51      Spill(season)   amount of water sent over the spllway (acft)
  52  ;
  53  Variables
  54      TCOST           total operating costs ($ continuous)
  55  ;
  56   
  57  Binary Variables
  58      I(watersource)  whether or not to use the water source (binary)
  59  ;
  60   
  61  Equations
  62      ObjectiveFunct         sum of all operating costs and returns
  63      WaterDemand(season)    how much water needed to irrigate
  64      SourceUse(watersource, season) where the water is going from the reservoir
  65      PumpCapacity(season)   max amount of water the pump can deliver
  66      PumpWaterAvailability(season)   how much water the pump has available to pump
  67      StorageS1              water balance for season 1
  68      StorageS2              water balance for season 2
  69      DamCapacity(season)    how much water each dam can store
  70      SpillLimit(season)     how much water the dam needs to spill to the river
  71      HighorLowDam           which dam (high or low) will be built
  72  ;
  73   
  74  ObjectiveFunct.. TCOST =E= Sum(farmland, Return(farmland) * A(farmland)) -
  75                         Sum(watersource, InitialCost(watersource) * I(watersource) + OperatingCost(watersource) *
  76                         Sum(season, X(watersource, season)));
  77  WaterDemand(season)..  Sum(watersource, X(watersource, season)) =G=Sum(farmland, IrrigationDemand(season) * A(farmland));
  78  SourceUse(watersource, season).. X(watersource, season) =L= MaxCapacity(watersource) * I(watersource);
  79  PumpCapacity(season).. X('riverpump', season) =L= 2.2 * 182.5 * I('riverpump');
  80  PumpWaterAvailability(season).. X('riverpump', season) =L= 2 * 182.5 + Spill(season);
  81  StorageS1..            S('season1') =E= WaterSupply('season1')- X('lowdam','season1')- X('highdam','season1')- Spill('season1');
  82  StorageS2..            S('season2') =E=S('season1')+ WaterSupply('season2')- X('lowdam','season2')- X('highdam','season2')- Spill('season2');
  83  DamCapacity(season)..  S(season) =L= MaxCapacity('lowdam') * I('lowdam') + MaxCapacity('highdam') * I('highdam');
  84  SpillLimit(season)..   Spill(season) =L= WaterSupply(season) + S(season);
  85  HighorLowDam..         I('lowdam') + I('highdam') =L= 1;
  86   
  87  Model HW5Reservoir /ALL/;
  88   
  89  * 6. Solve the Model as a Mixed Integer Program
  90  Solve HW5Reservoir using MIP Maximizing TCOST;
  91   
  92  * 7. Display the decision variable values in the list file
  93  Display X.L, I.L, A.L, S.L, Spill.L, TCOST.L;
  94   
  95  * send input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
  96  Execute_Unload "HW5Reservoir.gdx";
  97  * send the gdx file to excel
  98  Execute "gdx2xls HW5Reservoir.gdx"


